Key methodologies include analyzing the New Immigrant Survey (NIS) and additional data from the Mexican Migration Project and the Latin American Migration Project. By comparing wage gains at migration with GDP per worker differences, the study estimates that physical capital and total factor productivity (TFP) account for 38% of income differences, leaving 62% attributable to human capital. The study also considers the imperfect substitution between skilled and unskilled labor, finding larger wage gains for less educated immigrants, further indicating the significant role of human capital.

The authors conclude that human capital's share in development accounting is between one-half to two-thirds, with a point estimate of 60% under certain assumptions. This finding contrasts with earlier studies that estimated a lower share for human capital, suggesting prior biases due to selection issues. The study also explores skill transferability, noting that many immigrants experience occupational downgrading, potentially understating post-migration wage gains. Adjusting for this, the human capital share might lower to around one-half. Overall, the research emphasizes the substantial role of human capital in explaining income differences between countries.

